Key Concept
------------
1)filter() → keeps/removes elements (needs boolean)
2)map() → transforms elements (Employee → String)

Remembering notes
1. Do I need to remove data? → filter
2. Do I need ordering? → sorted
3. Do I need one result? → max/min/findFirst

| Type               | Methods                                        |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Stream methods     | `filter`, `map`, `skip`, `sorted`              |
| Optional-returning | `findFirst`, `findAny`, `max`, `min`, `reduce` |
| Optional methods   | `orElse`, `ifPresent`, `map`                   |

Intermediate operator -> filter, map, flatMap, sorted, distinct,limit, skip, peek
Terminal Operator -> forEach, collect, count, findFirst, findAny, max, min, reduce, anyMatch, allMatch, noneMatch
Optional Operator -> orElse, orElseGet, orElseThrow, ifPresent, map, filter

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) These is only used when natural ordering is required.
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())

2) When we need, custom sorting based on employee we can go for comparator.
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary).reversed())